    Character Creation

    1 – Choose a profession and a race appropriate to the game session's setting. 

    A profession is your character's primary occupation. 
    For example: Newspaper Carrier, Laborer, Servant, or Revolutionary. 
    Profession options may be provided by your GM (Game Master/Host). 

    It is suggested that you choose one of the well-known sentient races inhabiting the explored regions of space:
    Human / Dwarf / Unathi / Slime / Dionaea / Vox. 
    Other races and racial ability score bonuses may be made available at the GM's discretion. 

    2 – Determine your ability modifiers. 
    Ability scores reflect a character's natural strengths and weaknesses, and are represented by modifiers that will be added to your dice rolls. 

    Modifiers: +3, +2, +1, 0, -1, -2
    Abilities: Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, Charisma

    3 – Determine your character archetype. 
    Much like ability scores, archetypes indicate what you are good at—and what you aren't—but in a more abstract form. 
    As with the step above, assign modifiers to the archetypes listed below. 

    Modifiers: +3, +2, +1, 0, -1, -2
    Archetypes: Warrior, Rogue, Explorer, Sage, Artist, Diplomat

    4 – Grant yourself 10 points of Resolve. 
    Resolve reflects a combination of physical and mental resilience—a character's sheer will to survive. 
    Further details on how Resolve works can be found in the "How to Play" section. 

    5 – Choose a name and determine your character's physical appearance. 

    6 – Select basic gear appropriate to your Race and Profession. 

    Basic gear consists of a kit or set of simple items or tools. For example, someone with an Engineering Profession might possess a tool belt, a fire extinguisher, and similar items. 
    The appropriateness and scope of the items you may possess must be agreed upon with your GM. 

    How to Play

    1 – Begin the Quest. 

    2 – To attempt an action, describe what you wish to do, and then—only if the GM requests it—perform a Success Check. 

    Success Check = 1d20 Roll Result + Attribute + Archetype vs. Difficulty Class (5/10/15/20/25/30, at the GM's discretion). 
    In the event of a failure, the GM may enact a Reaction against you (take an action or increase the difficulty of a subsequent check). 

    3 – If something attacks you or otherwise acts against you, perform a Success Check to defend yourself. 

    4 – If you take damage, you lose 1 point of Resolve. If you deal damage, your target loses 1 point of Resolve. 

    5 – You may spend 1 point of Resolve to gain Advantage on a roll (roll 1d20 again and choose the higher of the two results) or to attempt an Extraordinary Feat (describe your highly unusual action; its success will be determined by the GM). 

    6 – If your Resolve drops to 0, you are knocked out. You regain 1 point of Resolve when you complete a Long Rest. 

    7 – The game ends once the Quest is completed! If you survive, you gain a new Ability. 
    Choose one of the following: +1 Attribute point, +1 Archetype point, or +1 to your maximum Resolve capacity.
